use the device is disconnected from the Inter- net). Accordingly, the authorizing par- ty does not warrant that the recipient will receive data of a particular quali- ty, fitness for purpose, or quantity, nor that the recipient will have a partic- ular legal position with regard to the data. Illustration: 57. Provider M provides a messenger application and service ‘for free’. In return, users authorize the processing of personal data on the device on which the application is installed for a variety of purposes that are in M’s commercial interest. In this passive access situation, the users are under no obligation actu- ally to use the messenger service, to produce a minimum quantity of user-generated data, or to produce data of a particular quality (e.g. data that reveals the actual identi- ty of the individuals with whom the users correspond). Users are also free to delete the application, thus making any further access to the data source on the part of M impos- sible. c. Duties with respect to third parties. Unlike the contracts described in Principles 7 to 9 above, where the Principle 10 ALI-ELI PRINCIPLES FOR A DATA ECONOMY"